commits.message:
Fix timeout handling when computing raw state delta (#3248)

Fixes #3242

### Description

We have seen an example of a TF provider `timescale/timescale` which
when bridged dynamically via `terraform-provider` will generate
resources that have a resource property input `timeouts` and output
`timeouts`.

[See full schema of bridged
timescale/timescale@2.6.0](https://gist.github.com/Zaid-Ajaj/fd88ff74ff3040cde084eda6931ad5bd)


When trying to create a resource using timeouts as follows: 
```cs
var service = new Pulumi.Timescale.Service("my-timescale-service", new ()
{
    // Configure your Timescale service here
    Name = "my-timescale-service",
    MemoryGb = 2,
    MilliCpu = 500,
    RegionCode = "us-east-1",
    Timeouts = new ServiceTimeoutsArgs
    {
        Create = "15m",
    }
}, new CustomResourceOptions
{
    Provider = provider
});
```
The bridge will actually call `Create`, provision the resource
successfully but then fail at the end when trying to compute the raw
state delta. See full error in issue description but the most important
part of the error is this:
```
Detail: rawStateRecoverNatural cannot process Object values due to map vs object confusion
```
This specific error happens because the bridge encountered `timeouts: {
create: "5m" }` in the outputs but raw state delta information for it is
_empty_: we handle `timeouts` in a special way and set their delta to
empty.

This PR changes the empty definition to a non-empty one but is NO-OP:
```diff
- RawStateDelta{}
+ RawStateDelta{Obj: &objDelta{PropertyDeltas: map[resource.PropertyKey]RawStateDelta{}}}
```
This solves the error coming from `rawStateRecoverNatural` but now when
recovering the state, we do get raw state but it doesn't match up with
original TF state because we remove `timeouts` before we compute the
delta causing an issue:
```
recovered raw state does not byte-for-byte match the original raw state
```
This is handled by conditionally removing `timeouts` ONLY when they are
not part of the data model of the terraform resource:
```go
var rawState rawstate.RawState
if _, hasTimeouts := outMap["timeouts"]; hasTimeouts {
	// if the outputs include timeouts, then they are part of the data model,
	// and we should not remove them so that raw state matches the outputs.
	rawState = newRawStateFromValue(schemaType, v)
} else {
	// otherwise, timeouts gets injected by terraform which we then remove from the raw state
	rawState = newRawStateFromValue(schemaType, v.Remove("timeouts"))
	if delta.Obj != nil {
		delete(delta.Obj.PropertyDeltas, "timeouts")
	}
}
```

Tested this locally against the `timescale` provider and confirmed that
it works as expected 🎉

~However, I cannot for whatever reason come up with a unit test that is
capable of returning `timeouts` from a terraform resource, they are
always truncated. I've tried multiple version of the following test but
they never include timeouts in their outputs. I don't know if this is an
sdk-v2 / plugin-framwork difference or I could be missing something. cc
@VenelinMartinov @corymhall~

EDIT: added test verifies the correctness of the implementation

<details>
<summary>Test that passes, specific to the plugin-framework</summary>

```go
// TestTimeoutsHandlingInResourceAttributes that shows using timeouts as part of the outputs works as indented
func TestTimeoutsHandlingInResourceAttributes(t *testing.T) {
	t.Parallel()
	provBuilder := pb.NewProvider(
		pb.NewProviderArgs{
			AllResources: []pb.Resource{
				pb.NewResource(pb.NewResourceArgs{
					CreateFunc: func(ctx context.Context, req resource.CreateRequest, resp *resource.CreateResponse) {
						timeoutsPath := path.Root("timeouts")
						resp.State.SetAttribute(ctx, timeoutsPath, map[string]any{
							"create": "30m",
						})
					},
					ResourceSchema: rschema.Schema{
						Attributes: map[string]rschema.Attribute{
							"timeouts": rschema.MapAttribute{
								Optional:    true,
								ElementType: types.StringType,
							},
						},
					},
				}),
			},
		})

	prov := provBuilder.ToProviderInfo()

	program := `
name: test
runtime: yaml
resources:
    mainRes:
        type: testprovider:index:Test
        properties:
            timeouts:
                create: "30m"
`

	pt, err := pulcheck.PulCheck(t, prov, program)
	require.NoError(t, err)

	pt.Up(t)
}
```
</details>
